    "headword_normalized": "δρυοχοι"
  },
  "δρύπτω": {
    "inflected_forms": [],
    "definition": "δρύπτω. (ἀπο-, περι-.) To tear away or strip off: ἀπό\n   κε ῥινοὺς δρύφθη (would have had his skin stripped\n   off) ε426. Cf. Π324.--In mid. of reciprocal action\n   β153 (tearing each other's...).",
    "headword_normalized": "δρυπτω"
  },
  "δρῦς": {
    "inflected_forms": [
      "δρύες",
      "δρῦς",
      "δρυός",
      "πέτρης"
    ],
    "definition": "δρῦς, δρυός, ἡ [cognate with Eng. tree, doubtless the\n   orig. sense. Cf. δρίος, δρύϊνος, δρυμά, δρύοχοι,\n   δρυτόμος]. Pl. δρύες Μ132. Acc. δρῦς Λ494, Ψ118.\n1. An oak Λ494, Μ132, Ν389 = Π482, Ξ398, 414, Σ558,\n   Ψ118: μ357, ξ328 = τ297.\n2. A felled oak or a piece thereof: ξύλον δρυός Ψ328.\n   Cf. ι186, ξ12, 425.\n3. In phrase οὐκ ἀπὸ δρυὸς οὐδ’ ἀπὸ πέτρης Χ126: τ163,\n   an obscure proverbial expression. In X the sense\n   seems to be 'giving utterance to thoughts from any\n   source as they arise,' 'with unconsidered or idle\n   words.' In τ the sense seems to be 'for you are\n   sprung (from men) not from ...'",
    "headword_normalized": "δρυσ"
  },
